WEBVTT

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:15.420
From the dawn of civilization to the 21st century,

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:19.110
there's been one creature we've relied on

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:22.030
more than any other, the cow.

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:26.030
It's a relationship that has shaped our lives

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:27.650
in so many ways.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.120
We worship them.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:31.300
Yeah, yeah, good days.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:31.940
Study them.

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:35.550
The key to the cow's success lies here, the rumen.

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:36.850
Use them.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:39.740
You never really know quite how your day is going to turn out.

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:40.580
Show them.

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:43.710
You've got great udders on them is what he's going to be looking for.

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:46.390
Sometimes they can be utterly frustrating.

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:48.090
This is ridiculous.

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:50.560
Whether in fact or fiction.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:52.360
The story of Jack and the Beanstalk,

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.800
when Jack sells the family cow for magic beans.

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:56.620
The mother is appalled.

00:00:57.130 --> 00:00:59.890
We simply could not do without the cow.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:40.000
This is the animal we see grazing in a field as we drive along the highway.

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:52.890
They have a timeless quality, as if they have always been with us, and we have always known them.

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:56.160
In short, we take the cow for granted.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:01:59.660
John Webster.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.160
professor of animal husbandry, veterinarian and author,

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:07.470
has no doubts about the importance of the cow in human history.

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:12.380
The cow really is perhaps the most useful animal there is.

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:17.190
We think of food in the form of meat and milk and cheeses

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:18.350
and everything produced from milk.

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:20.840
In addition to that, the cow, of course,

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:23.490
gives us clothing and shoes from her leather.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.380
And over the course of history, it's been expected to work,

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:29.980
to plough the land, to grow the arable crops, and, of course...

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:33.040
Her dung makes excellent fuel and excellent fertilizer.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:37.180
You've got fuel, you've got fertilizer, you've got clothes, you've got food.

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:38.740
What more could you ask?

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:56.220
A new member joins the extended family of one and a half billion cattle on the planet.

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:59.710
Even before it's born, it is welcomed.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:10.500
the highly social herd. Despite big, wide-set eyes and excellent all-around vision, cattle

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:23.200
see only one-sixteenth as much detail as humans. They have an excellent sense of smell.

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:29.440
In the wild, the calf will hide from predators.

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:32.610
Its scent ensures its mother can find it.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:39.060
The bond is formed in the first few vital minutes of life

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.180
as the mother licks her calf dry,

00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:44.870
stimulating its circulation and helping it to its feet.

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:57.710
But this is no wild animal.

00:03:58.420 --> 00:03:59.940
It is a Holstein Frisian.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:04.940
a domestic dairy breed representing centuries of careful human selection.

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:16.880
How did this extraordinary partnership come into being?

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:23.800
And why did the lives and fortunes of cows and humans all over the world become so intertwined?

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:29.870
The story begins...

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:45.940
Thirty thousand years ago, deep within the earth, the first artists recorded the beginnings of the most important relationship between man and animal the planet has ever witnessed.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:56.380
Some archaeologists think cave paintings like these were created by shaman as magical invocations for a successful hunt.

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:07.090
One of the most common images is that of Boss Primigenius,

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:11.040
the now extinct auroch, the ancestor of the modern cow.

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:18.820
Weighing 2,000 pounds with deadly horns,

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:21.120
the auroch was a formidable beast.

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:46.480
But a single animal could provide the clan with more meat than they could eat,

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:50.210
as well as bones, sinew, and guts for making weapons.

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:55.440
It gave horn and hoofs for tools, and hide for clothes and shelter.

00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:59.980
The auruk was the Stone Age supermarket, giving our ancestors...

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.040
everything they needed for survival.

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:10.040
30,000 years later, cooking on an open fire

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:13.940
still touches a primal nerve and binds the tribe.

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:18.350
We share a meal with our neighbors.

00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:20.890
To me, it was a big difference.

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:23.290
And part of it's just that meat's cooked outside

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:25.130
in this kind of atmosphere.

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:26.090
You know what I like?

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:27.510
Medium-ish would be great.

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:28.450
Thank you very much.

00:06:28.670 --> 00:06:29.970
A barbecue taps...

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:40.380
into our hunter-gatherer past, but eating beef no longer requires a life-and-death battle

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:47.920
with a formidable adversary. 8,000 years ago, something extraordinary happened to change

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:59.510
all that. The first cows came out of the forests and savannas and into the fields. Whether

00:06:59.510 --> 00:06:59.850
they came

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:06.890
their own accord or were caught is still debated. But the combination of bovine brawn and human

00:07:06.890 --> 00:07:22.030
brains would change the destiny of both species. Domestication was our first step in taking

00:07:22.030 --> 00:07:28.140
control of nature. In harnessing the power of once wild animals, we would utterly transform

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:29.370
life on Earth.

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:38.640
Lori Wynne Carlson is the author of Cattle, An Informal Social History.

