David Cale has timed it perfectly, and with magnificently blooming udders, his prized

00:40:42.170 --> 00:40:44.410
cow has swept the opposition from the field.

00:40:49.690 --> 00:40:54.900
In the last 50 years, genetics and breeding have tripled the average dairy cow's milk

00:40:54.900 --> 00:40:55.300
production.

00:40:55.300 --> 00:40:59.380
A Wisconsin Holstein called Miranda Oscar Lucinda

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:04.340
produced nearly 68,000 pounds of milk in one 12-month period,

00:41:04.660 --> 00:41:07.660
enough for 650 bowls of cereal a day.

00:41:09.160 --> 00:41:11.260
The dairy business in America alone

00:41:11.260 --> 00:41:14.670
is worth an estimated $75 billion a year.

00:41:15.870 --> 00:41:17.910
The colossal increase of dairy production

00:41:17.910 --> 00:41:20.500
has been matched by an equally dramatic increase

00:41:20.500 --> 00:41:21.580
in beef production.

00:41:22.440 --> 00:41:25.440
Eric Schlosser is the author of Fast Food Nation,

00:41:25.760 --> 00:41:28.580
a controversial overview of America's eating habits

00:41:28.580 --> 00:41:29.540
in the 20th century.

00:41:30.570 --> 00:41:40.070
The slaughterhouses of Chicago at the turn of the century were what inspired Henry Ford to create an assembly line system for the automobile.

00:41:40.760 --> 00:41:49.390
So the assembly line really, as an idea in America, begins in the slaughterhouses, except they are disassembly lines.

00:41:50.010 --> 00:41:59.240
So a steer will walk into the building completely unaware of what comes next, maybe thinking that he's about to get on another truck to go on another trip.

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:02.630
and is suddenly stopped by a barrier.

00:42:04.390 --> 00:42:07.850
And at that point, someone shoots the steer in the head.

00:42:09.800 --> 00:42:12.490
This employee is called the knocker,

00:42:12.960 --> 00:42:16.160
who is literally knocking the steer unconscious.

00:42:16.970 --> 00:42:19.850
And then a chain is put around one of the hind legs

00:42:19.850 --> 00:42:21.870
of the steer, and the steer is suddenly lifted up

00:42:21.870 --> 00:42:26.640
in the air, at which point a sticker with a long knife

00:42:26.890 --> 00:42:29.470
will slice the artery in the neck.

00:42:30.120 --> 00:42:32.320
and the animal then bleeds to death.

00:42:33.710 --> 00:42:36.030
What's extraordinary about American slaughterhouses

00:42:36.030 --> 00:42:38.370
is the speed of production.

00:42:38.860 --> 00:42:45.080
300, 350, 400 cattle an hour sometime down this one line,

00:42:45.240 --> 00:42:49.540
and the workers are working at an incredible pace to keep up.

00:42:50.310 --> 00:42:53.470
It's almost like something out of the Charlie Chaplin film

00:42:53.470 --> 00:42:57.420
Modern Times, of workers being completely obedient

00:42:57.420 --> 00:42:59.430
to the production line and the machinery.

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:02.390
The cattle just never stopped coming.

00:43:13.130 --> 00:43:15.490
This is where most cattle come from,

00:43:16.100 --> 00:43:17.840
feedlots like this one in Colorado,

00:43:18.460 --> 00:43:21.560
where steers are fattened on grain before being slaughtered.

00:43:21.880 --> 00:43:27.240
This facility can house up to 120,000 animals.

00:43:29.070 --> 00:43:29.970
The beef from an average...

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:40.350
steer sells for around $1,900. When full, this feedlot holds over $230 million of retail product.

00:43:45.200 --> 00:43:50.660
Our food production has changed more in the last 40 years than in the previous 40,000

00:43:50.660 --> 00:43:57.080
through the industrialization, in particular, of livestock, of cattle. Cattle were being

00:43:57.080 --> 00:43:59.960
raised in the United States up until recently as they'd been raised.

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:06.480
for millennia essentially eating grasses out on the prairie and it's in

00:44:06.480 --> 00:44:12.440
the last 40 to 50 years that a feedlot system arose in which cattle were being

00:44:12.440 --> 00:44:19.190
fed grain and in particular corn and it's in the last 25 or 30 years that

00:44:19.310 --> 00:44:26.320
we've had this intense industrialization of cattle. The cow's miraculous stomach

00:44:26.320 --> 00:44:29.960
can cope with almost anything but the feedlot died.

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:35.020
The diet of corn, while extremely fattening, also makes the animals vulnerable to disease.

00:44:36.190 --> 00:44:42.430
The cow's rumen can become inflamed, and bacterial infections can spread through the damaged stomach wall,

00:44:42.530 --> 00:44:45.670
causing anything from liver abscesses to brain damage.

00:44:46.360 --> 00:44:54.370
To fight off infection, an estimated 3.7 million pounds of antibiotics are fed to cattle each year in America,

00:44:54.890 --> 00:44:58.050
almost a million pounds more than are given to sick humans.

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:06.990
In order to have 100,000 cattle penned up in one space, you have to manage them differently,

00:45:07.410 --> 00:45:09.210
you have to raise them differently.

00:45:10.390 --> 00:45:14.110
Antibiotics become crucial to prevent large-scale outbreaks of animal disease.

00:45:14.810 --> 00:45:21.450
If you look at how cattle live and eat in America now, for the most part, it's been completely

00:45:21.450 --> 00:45:24.750
revolutionized and there's nowhere else in the world that's raising cattle this way.

00:45:25.960 --> 00:45:29.960
Industrialized agriculture is really something that's very...

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:37.300
new it happened in the last 50 years when energy costs became cheap we could take the animals off

00:45:37.300 --> 00:45:40.840
the land we could bring the food to them by machine and we could take away the dung by machine

00:45:40.860 --> 00:45:45.640
it also became possible with the use of antibiotics which meant we could keep large numbers of animals

00:45:45.640 --> 00:45:50.230
together without killing the lot now both of those things are going to break down sooner or later

00:45:51.030 --> 00:45:55.350
the cheap energy isn't going to work so it's going to be cheaper for the cow to go out and

