helps give one a longer-term perspective.

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:05.750
and helps understand that the natural world moves slowly both in and being degraded and improving.

00:47:07.720 --> 00:47:12.960
The Lassiter philosophy is to recreate as natural a set of conditions as possible.

00:47:13.970 --> 00:47:19.420
This means starting literally at grassroots, trying to restore the prairie that once fed

00:47:19.420 --> 00:47:29.970
vast herds of migrating bison. Miles of fencing.

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:34.860
crisscross the Lassiter Ranch as part of a grass management system designed to

00:47:34.860 --> 00:47:40.510
prevent over-grazing. Much of this approach to grazing and handling our

00:47:40.760 --> 00:47:46.340
cattle on these pastures is an attempt to mimic the migrating herds of the past

00:47:46.340 --> 00:47:50.900
that were on on the Great Plains such as the bison. Since we have fences and

00:47:50.900 --> 00:47:56.440
we've contained the cattle we have to mimic that by moving them and again by

00:47:56.440 --> 00:47:59.920
trying to plan the grazing so that we we don't come back.

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:07.940
until all plants have been recovered. Foreman Eddie Stanko places great value

00:48:07.940 --> 00:48:13.070
on building trust between man and cow. The first thing that we do different is

00:48:14.360 --> 00:48:18.640
nobody else that I know of gentles our cattle like we do. You know when we wean

00:48:18.640 --> 00:48:22.160
our calves we go through the gentling process which calms them down and

00:48:22.160 --> 00:48:26.500
teaches them all to eat cake out of your hand as compared to the neighbors you

00:48:26.500 --> 00:48:29.520
drive by the fence and the cattle are taken off over the hill instead of

00:48:29.520 --> 00:48:29.860
standing.

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:34.480
still and letting you do whatever you want to. Come calving time, it's really, really important

00:48:34.480 --> 00:48:37.840
because you don't have to worry about the cow taking off over the hill while you're trying to

00:48:37.840 --> 00:48:42.580
catch a calf. And that's one of the things that nobody else that I know of does that.

00:48:43.540 --> 00:48:48.390
The Lassiters don't use pesticides. And although they don't have large predators,

00:48:48.410 --> 00:48:53.660
which other ranchers face, there's no shooting of coyotes or other so-called pests either.

00:48:54.750 --> 00:48:59.940
Dale leaves the welfare of his 1,500 cattle to the prairie, on which they will spend

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:07.200
their whole lives. The more one knows about the natural world, the longer a

00:49:07.200 --> 00:49:11.020
person spends in the natural world, such as the short grass prairie where we are

00:49:11.020 --> 00:49:18.710
here. The true miracle of what is happening here is quite astounding.

00:49:19.450 --> 00:49:24.980
Our role here as land managers, cattle managers, is simply to stay out of the

00:49:24.980 --> 00:49:29.780
way enough that the natural forces can come to play and maintain.

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:36.080
sustain this tremendous resource, which really requires very little input from us, except

00:49:36.890 --> 00:49:38.450
hopefully not doing the wrong thing.

00:49:38.750 --> 00:49:42.800
A lot of people, they get shook up about things, and if they just kind of sit still and let

00:49:42.800 --> 00:49:46.020
Mother Nature take its course, sometimes it's better off than trying to hurry things.

00:49:48.580 --> 00:49:54.040
But can places like the Lassiter Ranch provide a realistic solution to our insatiable demands

00:49:54.040 --> 00:49:55.820
for meat in the 21st century?

00:49:56.810 --> 00:49:58.110
Professor John Webster.

00:49:58.360 --> 00:49:59.940
Dale Lasseter's ranch is...

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:06.020
utopia for beef cows he has a market for this very high quality product it is the rolls royce

00:50:06.020 --> 00:50:10.260
of beef production and there are people who prepare to buy that but it will never replace

00:50:10.260 --> 00:50:15.370
the feedlot absolutely for the simple reason that not everybody can afford a rolls royce

00:50:16.260 --> 00:50:23.550
i'm sure that we will continue to have for urban societies quite a lot of industrial production

00:50:23.770 --> 00:50:29.500
But I think inevitably, in time, there will be a step back.

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:39.380
to the past, but to a more ecologically sound, more humane system that fully recognizes the

00:50:39.380 --> 00:50:41.360
value of the land and the value of the cow.

00:50:44.000 --> 00:50:50.970
There's no question that Dale Lassiter represents the latest evolution of man's relationship

00:50:50.970 --> 00:50:58.480
to cattle, and I think that what the Maasai do with their animals and how they treat their

00:50:58.480 --> 00:50:58.940
animals.

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:05.140
is very much analogous in an American context to Dale Lassiter and his practices.

00:51:12.650 --> 00:51:17.090
The Maasai might seem unconnected to the world of modern farming practices,

00:51:17.680 --> 00:51:21.820
but this is where our story began, and this is where it now returns.

00:51:22.280 --> 00:51:29.970
For 8,000 years, all over the world, humanity...

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:32.960
has been bound by our common dependence on the cow.

00:51:37.670 --> 00:51:40.670
For the Maasai, giving cattle to a neighbor in need

00:51:40.670 --> 00:51:43.750
is the ultimate expression of sympathy and friendship.

00:51:50.520 --> 00:51:52.760
Although apparently a world away,

00:51:53.320 --> 00:51:56.200
the news of the 9-11 attack on the Twin Towers

00:51:56.320 --> 00:51:59.440
soon carried to the distant pastures and campfires

00:51:59.440 --> 00:51:59.980
of the Maasai.

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:00.700
of the Maasai.

00:52:01.770 --> 00:52:05.030
The unimaginable events were carefully described.

